<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Aadeel Peerally is a product leader based in Berlin, working on payments platforms, marketplaces, medical learning tools and solar software."
  ogImage="/og/about.png"
>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      I am a product leader based in Berlin. My work has centred on products where software meets money, operations
      and data: payments platforms, marketplaces, learning tools for medical students and doctors, and software for
      solar developers.
    </p>

    <h2>Where I started</h2>
    <p>
      I studied Telecommunications Engineering at King's College London, graduating with an MEng in 2005. My early
      roles were in sales and customer support, before I moved into product at the Guardian.
    </p>

    <h2>Internal teams are customers too</h2>
    <p>
      Before I worked in product, I worked alongside the people who keep a product running. At WeSwap, I was Head of
      Operations. I led the customer support team, up to 11 agents, and the compliance and fraud teams.
    </p>
    <p>
      That work taught me that internal teams are customers too. A product has to serve the people who use it and the
      people who support it. Support agents need tools that triage incoming queries, answer simple requests
      automatically and give customers self-service options. Without them, agents move from one call or email to the
      next, with no time to listen.
    </p>
    <p>
      The compliance and fraud teams faced a different version of the same problem. They handled a high volume of
      paperwork and failed applications caused by incorrect customer information. Some customers waited more than a
      week for their accounts to open. When I became Chief Product Officer, simplifying identity checks and making
      them as self-service as possible was an obvious priority. Accounts were approved much faster, and the team had
      more time for complex cases.
    </p>

    <h2>Payments and platforms</h2>
    <p>
      From WeSwap, I moved to Equals, then to Delivery Hero and AMBOSS. Each company handled payments in its own way,
      shaped by how quickly it had grown.
    </p>
    <p>
      At Delivery Hero, I led the migration of 20+ countries onto one financial platform, working across regulatory,
      engineering and regional teams. At AMBOSS, we brought a fragmented payment set-up together into one system,
      which cut time-to-market for campaigns and new products from weeks to hours. Each move taught me something
      different about how much the right answer depends on the business around it.
    </p>

    <h2>Building something of my own</h2>
    <p>Alongside WeSwap and Equals, I co-founded TryMyKitchen, a self-serve marketplace for cooking classes.</p>
    <p>
      Before TryMyKitchen, cooking class hosts listed their classes on general experience and deal sites, where they
      were easily lost among other offers. To stand out, hosts had to pay higher commissions or sell at steep
      discounts. We built a dedicated self-service portal for hosts, combined with paid and organic marketing designed
      to put their classes near the top of search results.
    </p>
    <p>
      The marketplace grew to 8,000+ classes across Europe and North America, with partners including Jamie Oliver
      cooking schools and Heineken. My biggest lesson: a product succeeds only when it understands its customers' pain
      and expectations clearly.
    </p>

    <h2>Working with AI</h2>
    <p>
      At PVcase, I lead product for a new platform that brings solar engineering and commercial workflows together.
      Part of that work is an LLM-powered review workflow that turns expert review into structured compliance checks.
      It is released and gaining traction with customers.
    </p>
    <p>
      I owned the decision to keep a human in the loop. User interviews made it clear that our customers did not yet
      trust the output of AI workflows on their own. Human validation lets them use the automation while staying in
      control of the result.
    </p>
    <p>
      As a personal project, I built Arnie OS, a system of 10+ AI agents that automates product management tasks such
      as data synthesis, PRD scoping and reporting. I wanted a practical AI project to learn from, and I continue to
      learn through other private projects.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      I value a steady operating rhythm: structured discovery, written PRDs that are reviewed before work starts, and
      weekly reporting on delivery and adoption. I hire and coach product and engineering people, and I try to keep
      everyone, from engineers to leadership, aligned on why we are building something.
    </p>

    <h2>Languages</h2>
    <p>English (native), French (fluent), Spanish (intermediate) and German (basic).</p>
  </article>

  <ContactCTA />
</BaseLayout>

